    <p>Notice what unites the list: every role is defined by finding what is true, not by recommending what to do. That is deliberate, because Best Answer Hub keeps the Diagnostician distinct from strategy roles. Data science leads the group at 34% growth with a $112,590 median, and security analysis pays even more at $124,910, because in both the value is the same: an organization pays a human to tell it what is actually going on (<a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BLS, 2025</a>).</p>

    <div class="cav">The honest bit</div>
    <h2 id="ai">Does AI threaten people whose strength is diagnosis?</h2>
    <p>AI makes the Investigator strength more valuable, because it produces exactly the thing a Diagnostician exists to check. That is why Best Answer Hub tags diagnostic roles as amplified rather than at risk. Modern models are, in the words of Turing Award winner Judea Pearl, powerful curve fitting: they surface correlations at a scale no human can match, but a correlation is a question, not a cause (<a href="https://www.quantamagazine.org/to-build-truly-intelligent-machines-teach-them-cause-and-effect-20180515/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pearl, Quanta Magazine, 2018</a>). Someone still has to establish which pattern reflects a real cause, and that someone is the Investigator.</p>
    <p>The reliability gap makes the point concrete. McKinsey's 2025 survey found that inaccuracy is the AI risk organizations most often say they have already experienced, and that the strongest performers build explicit human checks to validate model output before trusting it (<a href="https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/quantumblack/our-insights/the-state-of-ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">McKinsey, 2025</a>). Research published in Nature in 2026 goes further, showing that language models are structurally prone to confident, plausible falsehoods because their training rewards guessing over admitting uncertainty (<a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10549-w" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nature, 2026</a>). The work of verifying the claim and tracing the cause does not disappear; it gets more important.</p>
    <blockquote>A correlation is a question, not an answer. <span class="accent">The Investigator is the one who asks what actually caused it.</span></blockquote>

    <div class="cav">The trap</div>
    <h2 id="shadow">What is the shadow side of the Investigator strength?</h2>
    <p>The Investigator's blind spot, in the Best Answer Hub Work Signature, is analyzing past the point where the decision was already clear. The same drive that finds the true cause can keep demanding more proof than the situation needs, because the diagnosis never quite feels finished. There is a classic study behind this. When researchers gave judges more and more information about a case, their confidence climbed from 33% to 53%, while their accuracy barely moved, from 26% to 28% (<a href="https://faculty.fortlewis.edu/burke_b/Senior/BLINK%20replication/Overconfidence.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Oskamp, Journal of Consulting Psychology, 1965</a>). More evidence bought more certainty, not a better answer.</p>
