    <p>The fix is not to analyze less, it is to know when good enough is enough. The economist Herbert Simon called this satisficing: searching until an answer clears the bar the decision actually requires, rather than chasing an optimum that costs more than it returns (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satisficing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Simon, 1956</a>). This is where the Diagnostician differs from the strategist: a Navigator's blind spot is planning the route forever, while an Investigator's is verifying the evidence forever. Best Answer Hub names the shadow so the strength stays sharp.</p>
    <div class="callout"><div class="h">How Investigators cover the blind spot</div><p>Match the depth of the dig to the stakes: verify hard when a decision is costly and hard to reverse, and satisfice when it is cheap and easily undone. Set a proof threshold before you start, so you know what "enough evidence" looks like, and treat being right too late as its own kind of wrong. The rigor is the asset; a sense of when to stop is the guardrail.</p></div>

    <div class="cav">Prove it</div>
    <h2 id="resume">How do you show Investigator strength on a resume and in interviews?</h2>
    <p>For an Investigator, the evidence is the diagnosis nobody else reached, so frame each line as a chain: the problem others treated as random, the cause you traced, the fix it enabled, and the result that followed. Best Answer Hub advises leading with the cause you found rather than the tools you used, because a line that says you traced a recurring failure to a single overlooked source shows judgment, where a list of software shows only exposure. Use real numbers where you have them, and never invent one.</p>
    <p>Interviews are where the strength shines, if you show your reasoning. The most persuasive thing a Diagnostician can do is walk through how they separated cause from coincidence on a real problem, because it demonstrates the exact discipline that is getting scarcer: the refusal to accept a pattern as proof. In a world where anyone can generate a confident answer, the person who can say "that is a correlation, and here is how I would test whether it is the cause" is the one worth hiring.</p>

    <div class="cav">Better together</div>
    <h2 id="pair">Which strengths pair well with Investigator?</h2>
    <p>Investigator is most useful when its diagnosis turns into action. An Investigator paired with Builder means the fix addresses the real cause instead of the symptom, so less gets rebuilt for the wrong reason. An Investigator paired with Catalyst gets the finding acted on rather than filed, because a correct diagnosis that nobody moves on changes nothing. Because Best Answer Hub ranks all eight strengths, you can see which of these pairings is actually yours, and which strength you may need to borrow from a teammate.</p>

